mis=[('m1',0,'Magnesium reacts vigorously with cold water.','Magnesium reacts only very slowly with cold water; calcium, strontium and barium react more readily.','Compare magnesium with calcium.','research'),('m2',1,'A Group 2 oxide effervesces when added to water.','Oxide plus water makes a hydroxide; no gas forms. A soluble oxide may disappear into a colourless solution.','Barium oxide in water shows no bubbles.','ER 9701 w23 P21 Q2'),('m3',1,'A carbonate reacts with acid to make carbonic acid only.','Carbonate plus acid gives a salt, carbon dioxide and water.','Magnesium carbonate with hydrochloric acid releases carbon dioxide.','ER 9701 w22 P21 Q2'),('m4',2,'Heating a Group 2 nitrate gives the metal or nitrogen monoxide.','It gives the metal oxide, nitrogen dioxide and oxygen.','Balance oxygen after fixing the oxide and nitrogen dioxide coefficients.','ER 9701 w21 P21 Q2'),('m5',3,'Thermal stability decreases down Group 2.','Larger metal cations polarise the anion less, so decomposition needs more heat down the group.','Barium carbonate is more stable than magnesium carbonate.','ER 9701 w21 P21 Q2'),('m6',4,'Hydroxides and sulfates follow the same solubility trend.','Hydroxide solubility increases while sulfate solubility decreases down Group 2.','Barium hydroxide is soluble but barium sulfate is insoluble.','ER 9701 w23 P21 Q2')]
