q=[
(0,'Describe the reaction of magnesium with oxygen.','$2Mg(s)+O_2(g)\\rightarrow2MgO(s)$; magnesium burns with a bright white flame, forming a white oxide.'),
(0,'Describe the reaction of calcium with cold water.','$Ca(s)+2H_2O(l)\\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2(aq)+H_2(g)$; bubbles of hydrogen form.'),
(0,'Give an equation for magnesium with dilute hydrochloric acid.','$Mg(s)+2HCl(aq)\\rightarrow MgCl_2(aq)+H_2(g)$.'),
(0,'Give an equation for magnesium with dilute sulfuric acid.','$Mg(s)+H_2SO_4(aq)\\rightarrow MgSO_4(aq)+H_2(g)$.'),
(0,'Explain why strontium reacts more vigorously with water than calcium.','The outer electrons of strontium are farther from the nucleus and more shielded, so they are lost more easily.'),
(1,'Describe the reaction of barium oxide with water.','$BaO(s)+H_2O(l)\\rightarrow Ba(OH)_2(aq)$; the white solid dissolves to give an alkaline, colourless solution without effervescence.'),
(1,'Give an equation for magnesium carbonate with dilute hydrochloric acid.','$MgCO_3(s)+2HCl(aq)\\rightarrow MgCl_2(aq)+CO_2(g)+H_2O(l)$.'),
(1,'Give an equation for calcium hydroxide with dilute nitric acid.','$Ca(OH)_2(aq)+2HNO_3(aq)\\rightarrow Ca(NO_3)_2(aq)+2H_2O(l)$.'),
(1,'Give an equation for magnesium oxide with dilute sulfuric acid.','$MgO(s)+H_2SO_4(aq)\\rightarrow MgSO_4(aq)+H_2O(l)$.'),
(1,'Give an equation for calcium carbonate with dilute sulfuric acid.','$CaCO_3(s)+H_2SO_4(aq)\\rightarrow CaSO_4(s)+CO_2(g)+H_2O(l)$; the sulfate layer slows further reaction.'),
(1,'Describe the reaction of magnesium hydroxide with water.','It dissolves only slightly; the dissolved fraction produces hydroxide ions and an alkaline solution. It does not release a gas.'),
(2,'Give the balanced equation for thermal decomposition of calcium nitrate.','$2Ca(NO_3)_2(s)\\rightarrow2CaO(s)+4NO_2(g)+O_2(g)$.'),
(2,'Give the equation for thermal decomposition of magnesium carbonate.','$MgCO_3(s)\\rightarrow MgO(s)+CO_2(g)$.'),
(2,'Describe the products observed when magnesium nitrate is strongly heated.','White magnesium oxide, brown nitrogen dioxide gas and colourless oxygen gas form.'),
(2,'Explain the thermal stability trend of Group 2 carbonates.','Thermal stability increases down the group: the larger cation has lower charge density and polarises the carbonate ion less.'),
(2,'Predict whether barium nitrate needs a higher decomposition temperature than magnesium nitrate.','Barium nitrate needs a higher temperature because Group 2 nitrate thermal stability increases down the group.'),
(3,'Describe the change in reaction with water from magnesium to barium.','Reactions become faster and more vigorous down Group 2; magnesium reacts very slowly with cold water, while barium reacts readily.'),
(3,'Predict the relative ease of decomposition of strontium and calcium carbonates.','Strontium carbonate is more thermally stable and requires a higher decomposition temperature than calcium carbonate.'),
(3,'Explain the reactivity trend of Group 2 metals down the group.','Atomic radius and electron shielding increase, so the two outer electrons are lost more easily despite increased nuclear charge.'),
(3,'Compare the sulfate solubilities of magnesium and barium.','Magnesium sulfate is much more soluble in water than barium sulfate; sulfate solubility decreases down Group 2.'),
(3,'Predict the relative solubilities of calcium and strontium hydroxides.','Strontium hydroxide is more soluble in water than calcium hydroxide because hydroxide solubility increases down Group 2.'),
(4,'State the trend in solubility of Group 2 hydroxides.','Solubility increases from magnesium hydroxide to barium hydroxide.'),
(4,'State the trend in solubility of Group 2 sulfates.','Solubility decreases from magnesium sulfate to barium sulfate.'),
(4,'Explain why barium oxide gives a more alkaline saturated solution than magnesium oxide.','Their oxides form hydroxides in water; barium hydroxide is much more soluble, giving a higher hydroxide-ion concentration.'),
(4,'Predict whether barium sulfate or magnesium sulfate precipitates more readily from comparable solutions.','Barium sulfate precipitates more readily because it is far less soluble.'),
(4,'Describe the observation when dilute sulfuric acid is added to barium hydroxide solution.','A white precipitate of barium sulfate forms: $Ba(OH)_2(aq)+H_2SO_4(aq)\\rightarrow BaSO_4(s)+2H_2O(l)$.')]
